    [tag body/ELEMENT cls=tag] text«[body]» after«The ‘mean’ is another word for the ‘average’. It refers to the value y»
    [black] «To find the mean of a data set, there are two steps.»
    [black] «Step 1: Add up all the values.»
    [black] «Step 2: Divide that total by the number of values in the data set.»
    [black] «For example:»
    [black] «The lengths of five animals are 36 cm, 22 cm, 89 cm, 54 cm and 64 cm»
    [black] «Our data set is: {36, 22, 89, 54, 64}»
    [black] «Step 1: Add 36 + 22 + 89 + 54 + 64 = 265»
    [black] «Step 2: Divide – we have **five** pieces of data, so we will be dividing by **five** 265 ÷»
    [black] «This tells us the **mean** for this data set is 53 cm.»
    [black] «If the lengths of these five animals could be evenly shared, each animal would be 53 cm lo»
    [tag slide n/SUBTAG cls=tag] text«[Slide 4]» after«»
    [tag h5/ELEMENT cls=tag] text«[H5]» after«Median»
    [black] «The median is the **middle** number in a data set. It is the number that splits the data s»
    [black] «To find the median of a data set, the numbers **must** be in order from smallest to larges»
    [black] «For example:»
    [black] «The lengths of five animals are 36 cm, 22 cm, 89 cm, 54 cm and 64 cm»
    [black] «This data set is: {36, 22, 89, 54, 64}»
    [black] «This data set is **not** in numerical order, so that is our first step.»
    [black] «Step 1: Organise the data into numerical order. {22, 36, 54, 64, 89}»
    [black] «Step 2: Locate the value in the middle by making sure there is an equal number of members »
    [black] «The **median** length of these five animals is 54 cm.»
    [tag slide n/SUBTAG cls=tag] text«[Slide 5]» after«»
    [tag h5/ELEMENT cls=tag] text«[H5]» after«Trickier medians»
    [black] «Sometimes, finding the median requires another step.»
    [black] «When the data set has an odd number of values, you will always have a clear middle number.»
    [black] «However, when the data set has an even number of values, you will have two middle numbers.»
    [black] «For example:»
    [black] «The heights of eight animals are 74 cm, 88 cm, 153 cm, 62 cm, 10 cm, 47 cm, 68 cm and 177 »
    [black] «This data set is: {74, 88, 153, 62, 10, 47, 68, 177}»
    [black] «Step 1: Organise the data set into numerical order. {10, 47, 62, 68, 74, 88, 153, 177}»
    [black] «Step 2: Locate the values in the middle by making sure there is an equal number of members»
    [black] «Step 3: Find the mean (average) of these two numbers.»
    [black] «1. Add the numbers. 68 + 74 = 142»
    [black] «1. We have two values so divide by two. 142 ÷ 2 = 71»
    [black] «The **median** height of these animals is 71cm.»
    [tag slide n/SUBTAG cls=tag] text«[Slide 6]» after«»
    [tag h5/ELEMENT cls=tag] text«[H5]» after«Mode»
    [black] «The mode is the most common value. It is the number that is repeated most often.»
    [black] «If all members of a data set occur the same number of times, that data set has NO MODE.»
    [black] «For example:»
    [black] «In the data set {4, 7, 12, 54, 82, 88} each number occurs only once, so there is NO MODE.»
    [black] «In the data set {4, 7, 7, 12, 54, 82, 88} the number 7 occurs more than the other numbers »
    [black] «You might also come across a situation where there is more than one mode.»
    [black] «For example:»
    [black] «In the data set {4, 4, 4, 7, 12, 12, 12, 54, 82, 82, 88} you can see the numbers 4 AND 12 »
